00:00:01.470 --> 00:00:08.310 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  as the developer today you can launch  solutions faster than ever  building on the capabilities of google  cloud platform  your custom services and third-party  apis  but as more and more services are  integrated your solutions can increase  the complexity becoming hard to  understand and even harder to develop  debug and visualize you could write  custom services to manage these  integrations and host them in manually  maintained virtual machines  or link them together with a carefully  crafted event driven architecture  even with event driven architecture  observing the progress of a workflow  execution  is nearly impossible especially when a  workflow might run for hours  days or even months implementing error  handling can be difficult when something  fails along the chain of events  it becomes burdensome to track down plus  chaining services together gives you a  growing distributed system  with less predictable behavior over time  changes to one workflow may break  another workflow  also using those events without a  central definition of the entire  workflow it can be hard to see the full  picture and make appropriate changes  so how can you best use the power of  different services in your custom  workflow without all of these issues  by using google cloud workflows cloud  workflows is a fully managed service  that gives you the power to integrate  microservices  and apis while also error handling and  streamlining future development  from one central place you can describe  steps to combine custom services google  cloud products and anything  with http apis into an automated  workflow  and because it's serverless workflow  skills meet your demand  so you only pay for what you use so how  does it  work let's look at the two primary use  cases  event driven workflows and batch jobs  event driven workflows execute when a  certain trigger happens  for example let's say one of your  ecommerce customers has just placed an  order and they need their invoice  a new ecommerce event order kicks off  the workflow  first up a cloud run app receives data  from the event  and generates a pdf then the pdf is  passed to a cloud function that encrypts  and compresses it into a zip file  and finally the pdf is sent to a  third-party email api  sending the invoice to the customer  batch workflows consist  of running large jobs potentially on a  regular basis  for example a nightly job to check on  any incomplete orders from your  ecommerce site  each night at midnight the workflow is  kicked off the database is queried for  any incomplete orders in the past 24  hours  each valid account is sent to a cloud  function that can generate a  personalized offer  the offer is sent to a separate function  to generate an email  and those emails are sent using the same  third party api  in both cases workflow gives you a  holistic approach to workflow  management from the user interface or  from your favorite editor  you can build custom workflows and  investigate ones that are already  running  when calling other google cloud services  workflow handles all the underlying  details  like transport authentication and  waiting for long-running operations  google cloud iam makes it even easier to  manage permissions so you don't need to  worry about unauthenticated requests  workflow also gives you full visibility  into what's happening you can see each  step  execute as your workflow handles errors  retries and recovers from failures  even for third party apis plus workflow  executes with low latency  and no cold start with fully managed  scaling making them ideal for event  driven tasks  if your workflow is a batch  orchestration workflow for data  engineering or etl  you may want to look at google cloud  composer built on apache airflow with  data analysis focused integration  it's built for workflows involving data  analysis on the other hand if your  workflow is focused on orchestrating  work done by services  cloud workflow delivers a fully managed  easy to use solution  bring your mission critical workflows to  google cloud workflows to discover how  quickly you can deploy  powerful solutions while knowing  everything will scale  operate reliably in a fully managed cost  effective  environment check out cloud.google.com  workflows to learn more and to try it  out yourself  [Music]

